int searchCode         int value;            value        searchCode






















































Rule 1: No * in the parameter type

Example:

void search(int searchCode);

Declare: int value;
Pass: search(value);
Use inside the function: searchCode

A plain value is passed directly.

Rule 2: One * in the parameter type

Example:

void update(int *currentSize);

Declare: int currentSize;
Pass: update(&currentSize);
Use inside the function: *currentSize

A pointer receives an address. Use & to pass the address of a normal variable and * to access the value through the pointer.

Rule 3: An array parameter

Example:

void display(Medicine registry[]);

Declare: Medicine record[MAX];
Pass: display(record);
Use inside the function: registry[i].code

The array name is passed without &. In a function parameter, Medicine registry[] is adjusted to a pointer to Medicine.

Rule 4: A pointer to a structure

Example:

void displayMedicine(const Medicine *medPtr);

Declare: Medicine temp;
Pass: displayMedicine(&temp);
Use inside the function: medPtr->code

Use -> to access a structure member through a pointer. The const qualifier prevents modification of the structure through this pointer.

Rule 5: An array of pointers

Example:

void collectResults(Medicine *results[]);

Declare: Medicine *results[MAX];
Pass: collectResults(results);
Use inside the function: results[count] = ...

Each element of results is a pointer to a Medicine structure. Assign a valid structure address to an element.

Rule 6: A string parameter

Example:

void findBook(const char *isbn);

Declare: char isbn[21];
Pass: findBook(isbn);
Use inside the function: isbn

A character array name can be passed directly as a string pointer. The array must contain a null-terminated string.

Final Shortcut to Remember
Plain variable: Pass the variable directly.
Pointer parameter (*): Usually pass an address using &.
Array parameter ([]): Pass the array name without &.
Structure pointer (struct *): Pass &structureVariable; use -> inside the function.
Pointer to a value (int *): Pass &variable; use *pointer to access the value.
String parameter (const char *): Pass the character array name.
Array of pointers (Type *array[]): Pass the array name; each element stores a pointer.

Important: Count the stars to identify pointer levels, but also read the complete type. An array of pointers (Medicine *results[]) is different from an array of structures (Medicine registry[]).
